Title:
C/C++ checklist challenges solved (Linux ping + Windows driver registry handler)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post walks through two C/C++ security checklist challenges: a Linux “ping” utility and a Windows WDF driver registry handler. The Linux example shows how input validation can be bypassed via `inet_aton` accepting trailing garbage and `inet_ntoa` returning a pointer to a shared global buffer, enabling logic bypass and command injection into `system()`. The Windows driver example analyzes an attacker-controlled registry path and unsafe use of `RtlQueryRegistryValues` with `RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_DIRECT` but without `RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_TYPECHECK`, leading to registry type confusion. It explains how Windows 8+ mitigates untrusted-hive direct queries with a bugcheck (turning some cases into local DoS), then demonstrates how writable keys in trusted hives can re-enable exploitation. The post develops escalation paths from type confusion into stack overwrites and kernel write primitives using `REG_QWORD`, `REG_SZ` (UNICODE_STRING confusion), and `REG_BINARY` (length-driven overwrite). It’s useful for secure C/C++ developers, driver developers, red teamers researching LPE primitives, and defenders auditing kernel code patterns.

Technical Focus:
- `inet_aton` parsing quirks (trailing garbage acceptance) and validation bypass
- `inet_ntoa` static/global buffer aliasing and logic flaws
- Command injection via `system()` construction
- Windows kernel registry access via `RtlQueryRegistryValues` / `RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_TABLE`
- `RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_DIRECT` vs callback mode; missing `RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_TYPECHECK`
- Registry type confusion exploitation (`REG_DWORD`/`REG_QWORD`/`REG_SZ`/`REG_BINARY`) leading to stack overwrite / write-what-where primitives

Use Cases:
- Audit C/C++ network utilities for parser edge cases and static-buffer API misuse
- Identify and exploit/mitigate Windows driver registry type confusion patterns
- Hunt for writable locations in trusted registry hives to turn DoS into LPE
- Build detection rules/code review checks for dangerous `RtlQueryRegistryValues` flag combinations
- Create secure coding guidance/checklists and LLM-assisted review prompts for C/C++

Keywords:
Trail of Bits, C/C++ security checklist, secure code review, inet_aton, inet_ntoa, static buffer, global buffer, command injection, system(), SSRF check bypass, Windows driver, WDF, RtlQueryRegistryValues, RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_DIRECT, RTL_QUERY_REGISTRY_TYPECHECK, registry type confusion, REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, REG_SZ, REG_BINARY, UNICODE_STRING, kernel stack overwrite, local privilege escalation, KERNEL_SECURITY_CHECK_FAILURE, MS11-011, Mandatory Integrity Control, DACL, SACL, trusted hives, NtObjectManager, Get-AccessibleKey, write-what-where primitive